# PATCH: 005-disable-printing
#
# The DenBrowser build blocks printing at eight enforcement layers. Layers 1
# and 2 live in configuration; this source patch supplies layers 3 through 8:
#
# 1. PrintingEnabled=false enterprise policy (config/policies.json)
#    Removes standard print commands and shortcuts from browser chrome.
#
# 2. Locked print.enabled=false preference (config/mozilla.cfg and patch 017)
#    Keeps the supported runtime gate disabled and immutable.
#
# 3. PrintUtils front-end guards (toolkit/components/printing/content/)
#    Stop page setup, tab-modal preview, silent printing, and the native system
#    dialog before a dialog, static clone, or print promise is created.
#
# 4. nsGlobalWindowOuter::PrintOuter and internal Print
#    Block window.print() and direct content-process callers before delayed
#    print, modal, print-service, cloning, or IPC state is initialized.
#
# 5. CanonicalBrowsingContext::Print and PrintWithNoContentAnalysis
#    Reject parent-process print promises before content analysis, static
#    cloning, RemotePrintJob construction, or PBrowser print IPC.
#
# 6. WebExtension tabs.saveAsPDF (browser/components/extensions/)
#    Returns "not_saved" before a file picker or output stream can be opened.
#
# 7. nsPrintJob::CommonPrint (layout/printing/)
#    Final source-level backstop. It follows the existing failure cleanup path
#    and settles direct progress listeners and remote print actors.
#
# 8. print.js init() (toolkit/components/printing/content/)
#    Last-resort direct-dialog guard; waits for SubDialog's managed close hook,
#    queues non-modal feedback, and closes without starting preview work.
#
# Dedicated DenBrowserPrintingChild/Parent actors carry only trusted blocked
# events from every content frame and preserve popup opener context. The stock
# Printing actor remains top-level-only. Patch 004's shared browser-chrome
# notification displays the message asynchronously for four seconds, with no
# prompt service, nested event loop, or modal browser-chrome state.


